Say what a video frame is when it reaches the compositor.

A 10-bit test video on YouTube played washed out on a tablet: the whole
picture grey, blacks not black. That is what a frame drawn with the wrong
range or transfer looks like, and nothing says which colour space a frame
carried or how it was drawn.

On OHOS, VideoResourceUpdater now logs, once per change, each frame's pixel
format, bit depth, storage, colour space (primaries, transfer, matrix,
range), its HDR metadata and the resource type it is drawn as;
and, for a frame decoded in software -- VP9 and AV1, since the hardware
decoder here handles only H.264 and HEVC -- what it was uploaded as: YUV
planes in the channel format the GPU has for its bit depth, or RGB
converted on the CPU, and in which colour space.

The hardware decoder (overlay/media/gpu/ohos/ohos_video_decoder.cc) says
separately what AVCodecKit reports for its output beside what the frames
are labelled with.

Diagnostic; it comes out once 10-bit video is understood here.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.
